44
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:22,300
Before we can talk about quantum weirdness, we have to define what normal, boring, classical probability looks like.

45
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:28,040
We need a baseline to understand exactly what rules quantum mechanics will eventually break.

46
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:29,460
That's a great place to start.

47
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:33,180
So let's look at the probability simplex that's introduced in the first couple of chapters.

48
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:41,020
If I have a system, say a dice or a coin or just a state of knowledge about something, how does the math actually define that?

49
00:03:41,420 --> 00:03:50,700
Well, at its most basic classical level, a state of knowledge about any system is really just a list of probabilities for different distinguishable alternatives.

50
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:54,520
Okay. Like a 50% chance of heads, 50% chance of tails.

51
00:03:54,820 --> 00:03:59,920
Right. And mathematically, these probabilities are bound by two extremely rigid rules.

52
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:06,260
First, they have to be non-negative. You know, you can't have a negative 20% chance of a coin landing on heads.

53
00:04:06,620 --> 00:04:10,600
Yeah, that makes total sense. Negative probability is nonsense in the classical world.

54
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:16,340
Exactly. And second, they have to sum to exactly one because, well, something has to happen.

55
00:04:16,340 --> 00:04:22,020
If you add up the probabilities of all the possible mutually exclusive outcomes, you must get 100%.

56
00:04:22,020 --> 00:04:22,980
A value of one.

57
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:28,400
Right. And this collection of valid probability lists forms a geometric shape called a simplex.

58
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:32,560
Okay. So a state of knowledge is basically just a list of numbers adding up to one.

59
00:04:33,060 --> 00:04:35,140
But things change, right? Things move.

60
00:04:35,640 --> 00:04:44,120
If I want to transition from one state of knowledge to another, say I shake a box with a marble in it, how do we map that transition?

61
00:04:44,120 --> 00:04:52,240
So we use what are called stochastic maps. You can think of these essentially as matrices, you know, grids of numbers where every column sums to one.

62
00:04:52,880 --> 00:05:01,840
They represent transition weights. So if you are in state A, the matrix tells you the exact probability of moving to state B or C and so on.

63
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:06,920
But the geometry of the space gets incredibly interesting when you want to make these transitions reversible.

64
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:09,160
Reversible meaning I can undo the shake.

65
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:14,880
Right. To do that, the matrix must be bistochastic, meaning both the columns and the rows sum to one.

66
00:05:15,080 --> 00:05:17,840
Hold on. Let me make sure I'm visualizing this for everyone listening.

67
00:05:18,220 --> 00:05:21,500
A regular stochastic map is just moving forward in time.

68
00:05:21,660 --> 00:05:29,400
But a bistochastic map means you have a perfectly balanced system where you aren't losing the total probability weight in any direction.

69
00:05:29,660 --> 00:05:33,180
Precisely. You could, in theory, trace your steps backward.

70
00:05:33,180 --> 00:05:34,080
Okay, I follow you.

71
00:05:34,460 --> 00:05:39,140
And this leads us to a foundational piece of math called the Burkhoff von Neumann theorem.

72
00:05:40,100 --> 00:05:46,180
It states that the set of all these bistochastic matrices forms a very specific geometric space.

73
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:47,460
And that's the Burkhoff polytope.

74
00:05:47,620 --> 00:05:48,820
That's the Burkhoff polytope.

75
00:05:48,940 --> 00:05:49,040
Yeah.

76
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:53,160
You can visualize this polytope as a, like, a multidimensional jewel.

77
00:05:53,160 --> 00:06:00,640
And the extreme corners, the sharp vertices of this jewel, are perfect deterministic permutations.

78
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:02,760
Like shuffling a deck of cards perfectly.

79
00:06:03,100 --> 00:06:07,900
If I do a perfect Ferro shuffle, I am completely rearranging the deck, but I haven't lost any cards.

80
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:10,500
And no card is in a weird state of superposition.

81
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:10,980
Right.

82
00:06:11,240 --> 00:06:14,860
Every single card just moved to a new, exact, 100% certain position.

83
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:15,600
Exactly.

84
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:21,300
Permutation matrices are the extreme, reversible, information-preserving special cases.

85
00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:23,420
There were the sharp quarters of the jewel.

86
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:29,860
But most of the space inside this mathematical jewel isn't perfectly deterministic like that.

87
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:30,600
What is it then?

88
00:06:30,780 --> 00:06:34,640
The interior is made of convex mixtures of those permutations.

89
00:06:35,020 --> 00:06:40,040
And when you start mixing these pure states together, a mathematical concept called majorization,

90
00:06:40,380 --> 00:06:42,220
you trigger another crucial theorem.

91
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:43,840
The Hardy Littlewood-Polya theorem.

92
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:44,380
Yes.

93
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:45,520
Which says what, exactly?

94
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:51,520
It proves that mathematically, the act of mixing these states structurally forces entropy to increase.

95
00:06:51,620 --> 00:06:52,020
Okay, wait.

96
00:06:52,340 --> 00:06:55,400
I need an analogy for majorization and entropy increasing.

97
00:06:55,820 --> 00:07:00,660
Because convex mixtures in a polytope is, uh, it's a bit dense.

98
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:01,280
Fair enough.

99
00:07:01,380 --> 00:07:04,540
Let's think of classical probability, like mixing different colors of paint.

100
00:07:04,700 --> 00:07:07,220
You start with pure colors in distinct buckets.

101
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:10,900
Those are your extreme corners, your deterministic permutations, right?

102
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:11,080
Right.

103
00:07:11,140 --> 00:07:11,980
I like where this is going.

104
00:07:12,280 --> 00:07:13,560
So red is in bucket one.

105
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:14,800
Blue is in bucket two.

106
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:16,900
If I mix them together, I get purple.

107
00:07:17,360 --> 00:07:19,120
I can easily predict the resulting color.

108
00:07:19,460 --> 00:07:27,120
But if I just hand you a bucket of purple paint, you cannot unmix it to find the exact original shades of red and blue that I started with.

109
00:07:27,200 --> 00:07:28,140
You're stuck with the purple.

110
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:28,920
Exactly.

111
00:07:29,180 --> 00:07:31,520
It is an information loss machine.

112
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:34,860
That lost information is what we call entropy increasing, right?

113
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:36,740
That is a brilliant analogy.

114
00:07:37,060 --> 00:07:37,400
Yes.

115
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:49,560
And the profound takeaway here is that this Shannon entropy increase, this loss of information, isn't some extra physical law of thermodynamics that Flyxion had to invent or assume.

116
00:07:49,660 --> 00:07:50,360
It's just math.

117
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:54,340
It is a structural consequence of the geometry of majorization itself.

118
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:58,540
It is baked directly into the pure math of classical probability.

119
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:04,540
But if I'm playing devil's advocate here, and I know some of you listening are probably thinking this too, isn't this just classical physics?

120
00:08:04,680 --> 00:08:07,740
We're talking about paint mixing and shuffling cards and basic entropy.

121
00:08:08,140 --> 00:08:10,400
We promised a deep dive into quantum mechanics.

122
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:12,360
Where does the quantum stuff actually come in?

123
00:08:12,360 --> 00:08:17,360
The quantum stuff comes in precisely because this classical foundation is so airtight.

124
00:08:18,260 --> 00:08:21,360
Flyxion doesn't just invent quantum weirdness out of thin air.

125
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:25,200
Remember, the rule is constraint before content.

126
00:08:25,560 --> 00:08:25,700
Right.

127
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:31,820
We've established that classical probability works perfectly for a single static picture or a simple shuffle.

128
00:08:32,440 --> 00:08:40,540
The crack in reality, the actual quantum leap, happens when we try to figure out how these probabilities compose sequentially.

129
00:08:40,540 --> 00:08:43,340
Sequentially. Like doing one thing and then doing another.

130
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:43,920
Exactly.

131
00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:49,920
What happens when we chain transitions together using the actual underlying mechanics of waves?

132
00:08:50,160 --> 00:08:50,540
Ah.

133
00:08:51,060 --> 00:08:51,300
Okay.

134
00:08:51,440 --> 00:08:54,080
So if classical probability works, why change it?

135
00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:57,600
Because in the real world, things interfere with each other.

136
00:08:57,960 --> 00:09:02,180
This is where we look at composing transitions using amplitudes instead of bare probabilities.

137
00:09:02,320 --> 00:09:02,760
Exactly.

138
00:09:03,020 --> 00:09:06,500
In classical probability, we compose transitions at the probability level.

139
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:08,060
You multiply the chances together.

140
00:09:08,060 --> 00:09:13,340
But quantum mechanics, as we've observed in the real world in labs, composes at the amplitude level.

141
00:09:13,540 --> 00:09:15,300
So you manipulate the amplitudes first.

142
00:09:15,480 --> 00:09:15,760
Yes.

143
00:09:16,060 --> 00:09:21,720
You manipulate the amplitudes and then you square the final amplitude to get the probability we actually observe.

144
00:09:22,140 --> 00:09:24,560
And we need to explain why that matters to the math.

145
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:29,500
Because if I take two probabilities, say A and B, and I just add them, it's just A plus B.

146
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:36,340
But I take two amplitudes, A and B, add them together, and then square them to get the probability of basic algebra.

147
00:09:37,300 --> 00:09:42,640
Algebra tells us that A plus B all squared is not just A squared plus B squared.

148
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:43,140
Nope.

149
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:44,640
You get this extra piece.

150
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:45,400
Plus 2AB.

151
00:09:45,580 --> 00:09:48,360
And that plus 2AB is literally everything.

152
00:09:48,520 --> 00:09:49,520
That is the cross term.

153
00:09:49,700 --> 00:09:51,840
That cross term represents interference.

154
00:09:52,180 --> 00:09:53,760
Because amplitudes can be negative.

155
00:09:54,080 --> 00:09:55,520
That 2AB term can be negative.

156
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:58,720
Which means two probabilities can actually cancel each other out.

157
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:00,200
That is quantum interference.

158
00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:06,620
When you use classical probability level composition, you completely destroy and discard those cross terms.

159
00:10:06,760 --> 00:10:07,960
You're throwing away the interference.

160
00:10:07,960 --> 00:10:15,300
So to keep those cross terms, to allow for interference, we need a mathematical lift to handle the amplitudes underneath the probabilities.

161
00:10:16,260 --> 00:10:20,120
And this introduces us to two massive classes of matrices in the manuscript.

162
00:10:20,420 --> 00:10:22,140
Yes, the two main contenders.

163
00:10:22,580 --> 00:10:27,740
We have orthostochastic matrices, which are built using strictly real numbers, just normal numbers.

164
00:10:27,740 --> 00:10:36,920
And we have unistochastic matrices, which are built using complex numbers, numbers involving the imaginary unit, the square root of negative 1.

165
00:10:37,060 --> 00:10:37,260
Correct.

166
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:50,100
The manuscript starts by testing the simplest possible system, a 2 by 2 level, meaning a system with just two possible outcomes, say a coin flip or a single quibit measuring spin up or spin down.

167
00:10:50,100 --> 00:10:51,480
We call this n equals 2.

168
00:10:51,480 --> 00:10:52,520
Okay, n equals 2.

169
00:10:52,600 --> 00:10:56,100
And at n equals 2, the manuscript proves the degeneracy theorem.

170
00:10:56,620 --> 00:11:04,040
At this specific dimension, real numbers and complex numbers are mathematically identical in their ability to realize these transitions.

171
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:05,980
You literally cannot tell the difference.

172
00:11:06,080 --> 00:11:10,860
So a 2 by 2 real orthogonal matrix handles the interference and the probabilities perfectly.

173
00:11:11,100 --> 00:11:12,340
It handles it flawlessly.

174
00:11:12,340 --> 00:11:17,700
Okay, so if I live in a universe where everything only ever has two outcomes, real numbers work just fine.

175
00:11:17,780 --> 00:11:19,100
I don't need to imagine your numbers at all.

176
00:11:19,760 --> 00:11:23,540
But, and this is a big but, the universe obviously has more than two outcomes.

177
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:25,920
What happens when we hit n equals 3?

178
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:27,720
A 3 outcome system.

179
00:11:28,080 --> 00:11:31,740
This is where real numbers fail completely and spectacularly.

180
00:11:31,900 --> 00:11:33,240
And Flyxion proves this.

181
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:33,920
Yes.

182
00:11:34,240 --> 00:11:37,960
The manuscript uses a specific transition matrix as a test case.

183
00:11:37,960 --> 00:11:43,120
It's called the 3 by 3 discrete Fourier transform, or DFT, matrix.

184
00:11:43,780 --> 00:11:51,620
It is a completely valid, coherent transition where every single outcome probability is exactly one-third.

185
00:11:51,720 --> 00:11:55,000
Okay, let's break down exactly why real numbers choke on this.

186
00:11:55,580 --> 00:12:01,400
To get a probability of one-third everywhere using only real numbers, we have to remember the rule.

187
00:12:01,940 --> 00:12:03,700
Probability is amplitude squared.

188
00:12:03,700 --> 00:12:08,260
So, what real number, when squared, equals exactly one-third?

189
00:12:08,460 --> 00:12:13,700
Well, it has to be either positive 1 over the square root of 3 or negative 1 over the square root of 3.

190
00:12:13,820 --> 00:12:13,980
Right.

191
00:12:14,060 --> 00:12:16,780
Those are the only two real numbers that square to exactly one-third.

192
00:12:17,040 --> 00:12:25,740
So, to build this 3 by 3 matrix using only real numbers, every single entry in that 9 square grid must be one of those two specific values.

193
00:12:25,800 --> 00:12:27,040
Okay, so it's doable so far.

194
00:12:27,040 --> 00:12:33,120
But furthermore, because this represents a reversible, information-preserving transition like our perfect shuffle from earlier,

195
00:12:33,700 --> 00:12:36,960
the math dictates that the rows must be orthogonal to each other.

196
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:39,140
And orthogonal here means what, physically?

197
00:12:39,640 --> 00:12:43,940
It means they are perfectly independent, non-overlapping states.

198
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:52,100
Mathematically, it means if you take the dot product of any two rows, meaning you multiply their corresponding entries together and add them up,

199
00:12:52,460 --> 00:12:54,280
the result must be exactly zero.

200
00:12:54,280 --> 00:12:57,320
So, we are mathematically forced into a very tight corner here.

201
00:12:57,960 --> 00:13:01,960
We need a 3 by 3 grid of essentially positive and negative ones.

202
00:13:02,020 --> 00:13:09,780
If we pull out that scaling factor of 1 over root 3, where every row's dot product with every other row cancels out to zero.

203
00:13:10,180 --> 00:13:12,440
In math, this is called a Hadamard matrix.

204
00:13:12,580 --> 00:13:12,980
Exactly.

205
00:13:13,240 --> 00:13:20,660
We need a 3 by 3 real Hadamard matrix to make this perfectly valid one-third probability state exist using real numbers.

206
00:13:20,660 --> 00:13:26,080
But here is the brutal truth of the math, and it is a hard, impassable mathematical wall.

207
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:26,820
Pay it on me.

208
00:13:27,220 --> 00:13:33,200
You can program a computer to exhaustively check every single possible sign combination for a 3 by 3 matrix.

209
00:13:33,560 --> 00:13:37,860
There are 2 to the 9th power possibilities, so 512 total combinations.

210
00:13:38,340 --> 00:13:39,020
It's not that many.

211
00:13:39,160 --> 00:13:39,620
It's not.

212
00:13:39,760 --> 00:13:45,380
And if you check all 512, you will find that a 3 by 3 Hadamard matrix literally does not exist.

213
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:47,100
Wait, it just, it doesn't exist.

214
00:13:47,320 --> 00:13:48,320
Like, not at all.

215
00:13:48,320 --> 00:13:49,980
It does not exist.

216
00:13:50,180 --> 00:13:50,320
Right.

217
00:13:50,440 --> 00:13:54,940
The math proves they only exist for dimensions of 1, 2, and multiples of 4.

218
00:13:55,280 --> 00:13:58,960
There is no 3 by 3 real matrix that can perform this job.

219
00:13:59,300 --> 00:14:01,120
It is mathematically impossible.

220
00:14:01,480 --> 00:14:05,960
It's like trying to build a perfect enclosed triangle out of square Lego blocks.

221
00:14:05,960 --> 00:14:17,440
At a certain point, no matter how clever you are, the 2D real number pieces you have in your hand simply cannot physically snap together to form the 3D geometry the universe is asking for.

222
00:14:17,940 --> 00:14:19,600
The pegs and holes just do not align.

223
00:14:19,600 --> 00:14:20,940
That's a great way to picture it.

224
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:22,520
And this is a profound proof.

225
00:14:22,680 --> 00:14:24,960
It shows that complex numbers aren't just a neat trick.

226
00:14:25,200 --> 00:14:29,320
They aren't just a mathematical convenience that physicists use because they're lazy or want to look smart.

227
00:14:29,580 --> 00:14:29,740
Right.

228
00:14:29,960 --> 00:14:33,940
They are structurally necessary for any system where n is 3 or greater.

229
00:14:33,940 --> 00:14:42,180
The math is literally screaming at us that the real number line is simply too narrow to hold the information required to move between these three states coherently.

230
00:14:42,580 --> 00:14:46,880
We must have complex amplitudes to make the math work or the universe breaks.

231
00:14:46,880 --> 00:14:57,420
My mind is already melting, but the crazy part is we are just getting started on this deep dive because that was just for a single transition in a 3 outcome system.

232
00:14:58,240 --> 00:15:00,260
Let's retreat back to safety for a moment.

233
00:15:00,580 --> 00:15:01,920
What if we stick to n equals 2?

234
00:15:02,220 --> 00:15:07,060
We just established that for 2 outcomes, real numbers do work perfectly, right?

235
00:15:07,080 --> 00:15:07,420
They do.

236
00:15:07,800 --> 00:15:14,720
So what happens if we take these perfectly valid, saved 2 by 2 real number transitions and chain them together into a loop?

237
00:15:14,720 --> 00:15:18,840
This is a brilliant and honestly devious shift in the manuscript.

238
00:15:19,280 --> 00:15:24,000
We move from looking at a single edge, a single transition, to a context graph.

239
00:15:24,380 --> 00:15:27,300
Imagine a triangle made of three different measurement contexts.

240
00:15:27,500 --> 00:15:29,500
Let's call them z, x, and y.

241
00:15:29,660 --> 00:15:34,500
Okay, so three different ways to measure our 2 outcome system, a range and a sequence that loops back on itself.

242
00:15:34,580 --> 00:15:37,140
So it goes z to x, x to y, and y back to z.

243
00:15:37,220 --> 00:15:41,640
Right, and if you track the exact angles of these transitions in the underlying state space,

244
00:15:41,640 --> 00:15:45,600
they obey a strict geometric rule called the triangle gluing theorem.

245
00:15:45,920 --> 00:15:50,440
Because it's a closed loop, if you travel from z to x, then x to y, and finally y back to z,

246
00:15:50,640 --> 00:15:53,480
the exact closure demands they multiply to the identity.

247
00:15:53,860 --> 00:15:59,120
Meaning, if I walk around a triangle and end up on the exact same footprint where I started,

248
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:02,920
facing the exact same direction, I haven't changed.

249
00:16:03,340 --> 00:16:04,800
The net movement is zero.

250
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:05,160
Yeah.

251
00:16:05,180 --> 00:16:06,320
That sounds totally logical.

252
00:16:06,320 --> 00:16:13,360
It is logical for exact angles, but remember, in quantum mechanics, we don't observe the exact underlying angles.

253
00:16:13,540 --> 00:16:15,260
We only observe the probabilities.

254
00:16:15,900 --> 00:16:15,940
Wow.

255
00:16:16,180 --> 00:16:23,020
And the projection from those exact angles down to probabilities destroys a crucial piece of information.

256
00:16:23,780 --> 00:16:26,260
Specifically, it destroys a relative sine bit.

257
00:16:26,860 --> 00:16:29,540
How so? Walk me through that. I want to make sure I'm picturing this right.

258
00:16:29,540 --> 00:16:35,180
Sure. The transition probability between two states is given by the cosine squared of the angle between them.

259
00:16:35,460 --> 00:16:38,860
But when you square the cosine, you strip away the sine of the angle itself.

260
00:16:39,060 --> 00:16:40,220
You lose the directionality.

261
00:16:40,360 --> 00:16:44,140
You know the distance, but you don't know if it was a positive turn or a negative turn.

262
00:16:44,220 --> 00:16:44,800
Okay, I see.

263
00:16:44,860 --> 00:16:51,800
So I know how far apart things are, but I've lost the GPS coordinates of how they are oriented relative to each other.

264
00:16:52,040 --> 00:16:55,080
So what happens when we try to close the loop using only these probabilities?

265
00:16:55,080 --> 00:17:02,840
That is where the paradox hits. And the sharpest example Fleschen gives is the mutually unbiased basis, or MUB corollary.

266
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:06,020
Let's say you try to make these three bases mutually unbiased.

267
00:17:06,100 --> 00:17:07,480
And in plain English, that means what?

268
00:17:07,820 --> 00:17:11,740
It means the transition probability between any two of them is exactly one half.

269
00:17:12,420 --> 00:17:17,060
Geometrically, that translates to a 45 degree angle, or pi over 4.

270
00:17:17,180 --> 00:17:23,300
Okay, so z is 45 degrees from x, x is 45 degrees from y, and y is 45 degrees from z.

271
00:17:23,300 --> 00:17:30,780
Right. But if you try to build that specific loop using strictly real numbers in a two-dimensional space, you run into a brick wall.

272
00:17:31,340 --> 00:17:39,500
The math of real numbers demands that for the triangle to close and equal the identity, pi over 4 must algebraically equal either 0 or pi over 2.

273
00:17:39,680 --> 00:17:42,540
Which is a complete, unadulterated logical contradiction.

274
00:17:43,080 --> 00:17:47,000
45 degrees does not equal 0 degrees, and it does not equal 90 degrees.

275
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,200
This is the Escher staircase.

276
00:17:49,940 --> 00:17:54,160
If you've ever seen that M.C. Escher drawing where the monks are walking up the stairs forever,

277
00:17:54,660 --> 00:18:02,160
every individual step, every individual transition between two monks looks perfectly flat, normal, and mathematically valid.

278
00:18:02,620 --> 00:18:07,400
But when you pull back and look at the whole loop, the geometry is a paradox.

279
00:18:08,200 --> 00:18:10,040
It cannot exist in real space.

280
00:18:10,040 --> 00:18:18,360
That is a phenomenal visual, and if we connect this to the bigger picture, this failure is entirely distinct from the 3x3 failure we talked about earlier.

281
00:18:18,520 --> 00:18:18,920
How so?

282
00:18:19,040 --> 00:18:24,280
Well, the 3x3 DFT matrix was a local failure. A single matrix couldn't be built.

283
00:18:24,760 --> 00:18:29,360
But this triangle paradox is a global topological failure.

284
00:18:29,780 --> 00:18:32,460
Topological meaning it's about the shape of the connections.

285
00:18:32,460 --> 00:18:39,000
Exactly. In graph theory, we talk about the betty number, specifically betty number 1, or beta 1.

286
00:18:39,080 --> 00:18:42,260
Okay, you're going to have to translate betty number into English for me and the listeners.

287
00:18:42,480 --> 00:18:47,080
Of course. Think of it as simply counting the number of holes or independent cycles in a network.

288
00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:55,100
A straight line has a betty number of 0, but a triangle, because it forms one closed loop, has a betty number of beta 1 equals 1.

289
00:18:55,200 --> 00:18:55,760
Okay, got it.

290
00:18:55,760 --> 00:19:06,460
The manuscript proves unequivocally that local admissibility, meaning each individual step being mathematically valid on its own, does not guarantee global realizability.

291
00:19:06,460 --> 00:19:14,860
The whole universe has to make sense all at once, and when you introduce a cycle in real numbers, even for simple two-outcome systems, it doesn't make sense.

292
00:19:15,360 --> 00:19:18,080
The universe simply rejects real numbers in a loop.

293
00:19:18,080 --> 00:19:27,240
So to recap where we are, real numbers fail to handle three outcomes locally, and they fail to handle two outcomes if those outcomes form a closed loop.

294
00:19:27,440 --> 00:19:32,240
That is wild. We are being dragged, kicking, and screaming into the complex number field.

295
00:19:32,240 --> 00:19:40,040
We really are, and that forces us to look beyond single, isolated systems, because quantum mechanics is famous for how multiple systems interact.

296
00:19:40,420 --> 00:19:47,220
Entanglement, spooky action at a distance. How does the pure math dictate that two independent systems should compose?

297
00:19:47,220 --> 00:19:52,260
This takes us right into chapter 8, which explores composite systems and local tomography.

298
00:19:52,260 --> 00:19:58,900
We need to test our three main mathematical contenders, or three scalar fields, to see who survives system composition.

299
00:19:59,180 --> 00:20:02,440
So we have real numbers, complex numbers, and quaternionic numbers.

300
00:20:02,540 --> 00:20:06,920
Right. And to test them, we apply a principle called local tomography.

301
00:20:07,520 --> 00:20:10,920
This isn't a complex physical law. It's an operational idea.

302
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:18,400
It basically states that the joint state of two systems should be exactly as informative as the product of the local measurements.

303
00:20:18,960 --> 00:20:29,500
Meaning, if I measure my particle in my lab, and you measure your particle in your lab, and we get together and combine our data, we should have the complete 100% picture of the joint system.

304
00:20:30,080 --> 00:20:37,780
There shouldn't be any missing dimensions of information hiding in the shadows, nor should there be extra redundant dimensions muddying the waters.

305
00:20:37,780 --> 00:20:45,860
Precisely. To test this, the manuscript uses a dimension counting argument, which it credits to the physicists Araki and Hardy.

306
00:20:46,660 --> 00:20:51,400
Let's do the math slowly. Let's combine two two-dimensional systems, two qubits.

307
00:20:51,520 --> 00:20:54,020
All right, I have a qubit. You have a qubit. Let's count dimensions.

308
00:20:54,280 --> 00:20:58,480
The number of parameters needed to fully describe a state depends on the math field you use.

309
00:20:58,480 --> 00:21:02,540
Let's start with complex numbers, since that's what textbook quantum mechanics uses.

310
00:21:03,240 --> 00:21:08,440
Combining two complex qubits means multiplying their local dimensions, so 2 squared times 2 squared.

311
00:21:08,500 --> 00:21:10,500
Which is 4 times 4, so 16 parameters.

312
00:21:11,020 --> 00:21:12,760
That equals 16 parameters.

313
00:21:13,040 --> 00:21:20,440
Now, if you separately count the parameters required to build a joint, complex, 4x4 density matrix from scratch, it is exactly 16.

314
00:21:20,800 --> 00:21:21,880
It matches perfectly.

315
00:21:22,480 --> 00:21:25,020
Local knowledge multiplied equals global knowledge.

316
00:21:25,020 --> 00:21:29,560
Okay, so complex numbers pass the test. What about quaternions?

317
00:21:30,200 --> 00:21:34,640
For anyone listening who might not know, quaternions are like complex numbers on steroids.

318
00:21:35,100 --> 00:21:39,620
Instead of just one imaginary number i, they have i, j, and k.

319
00:21:39,780 --> 00:21:40,980
Right, they're more complex.

320
00:21:41,380 --> 00:21:44,060
For quaternionic numbers, the local dimensions are larger.

321
00:21:44,400 --> 00:21:50,440
If you multiply them together locally, you would require 36 parameters to describe the joint system based on that local knowledge.

322
00:21:50,500 --> 00:21:51,160
Okay, 36.

323
00:21:51,160 --> 00:21:57,780
But, if you look at the actual parameters of a joint quaternionic system of that exact size, it only has 28 parameters.

324
00:21:58,000 --> 00:21:59,460
36 is greater than 28.

325
00:21:59,720 --> 00:22:02,040
So the naive product overcounts by 8.

326
00:22:02,420 --> 00:22:06,740
It assumes the universe has 36 dials to turn, but the actual math only gives you 28.

327
00:22:06,940 --> 00:22:09,020
It's too restrictive, too rigidly connected.

328
00:22:09,200 --> 00:22:10,580
Exactly. It's overdetermined.

329
00:22:10,860 --> 00:22:12,300
Now, let's look at real numbers.

330
00:22:12,660 --> 00:22:15,440
The math field we evolved to understand and use every day.

331
00:22:15,440 --> 00:22:20,380
If you combine two real qubits, the product of their local parameters is 3 times 3, which is 9.

332
00:22:20,640 --> 00:22:24,000
Okay, 9 parameters, if I just multiply our local observations.

333
00:22:24,120 --> 00:22:30,020
But a joint 4x4 real density matrix actually requires 10 parameters to be fully described.

334
00:22:30,640 --> 00:22:32,140
9 is strictly less than 10.

335
00:22:32,380 --> 00:22:34,060
The product undercounts by 1.

336
00:22:34,460 --> 00:22:35,960
There is a hidden 10th dimension.

337
00:22:36,060 --> 00:22:38,520
Look, I want to make sure I grasp the gravity of this.

338
00:22:38,520 --> 00:22:39,440
Let's use an analogy.

339
00:22:40,140 --> 00:22:47,860
Imagine you and I are two detectives trying to solve a murder case, and we are each interrogating a different suspect in completely separate rooms.

340
00:22:47,940 --> 00:22:48,700
Okay, I'm with you.

341
00:22:48,880 --> 00:22:52,840
In the complex universe, if we come together and share our notes, we have the whole 16-piece puzzle.

342
00:22:53,380 --> 00:22:55,260
It just works.

343
00:22:56,140 --> 00:23:00,740
In the quaternionic universe, we share our notes and realize our facts contradict each other.

344
00:23:00,860 --> 00:23:01,660
It's a mess.

345
00:23:01,800 --> 00:23:03,600
Yes, and in the real universe.

346
00:23:03,600 --> 00:23:14,540
In the real universe, even if we share absolutely everything we observed in our interrogation rooms, there is a secret 10th clue, a 10th dimension of the crime that neither of us could see locally.

347
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:22,920
It only exists in the space between the suspects, and we can only see it if we somehow magically merged our brains together into a single global entity.

348
00:23:23,240 --> 00:23:25,100
That is exactly what the math means.

349
00:23:25,600 --> 00:23:29,640
That missing 10th dimension in real quantum mechanics isn't a rounding error.

350
00:23:29,640 --> 00:23:36,580
It means real composite systems carry extra correlational structure that simply cannot be seen by local observers.

351
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:37,100
Wow.

352
00:23:37,560 --> 00:23:40,120
Complex numbers are the unique Goldilocks field.

353
00:23:40,440 --> 00:23:41,840
Not too big, not too small.

354
00:23:42,260 --> 00:23:46,020
They are the only field where local and global dimensions match identically.

355
00:23:46,460 --> 00:23:50,420
But the manuscript doesn't just point at the missing 10th dimension in real numbers and walk away.

356
00:23:50,700 --> 00:23:52,260
It actually hunts it down.

357
00:23:52,360 --> 00:23:53,940
It puts a magnifying glass on it.

358
00:23:54,500 --> 00:23:58,380
Which brings us to a massive revelation in chapters 9 through 12.

359
00:23:58,380 --> 00:24:03,400
What exactly is this secret dimension hiding in the real number blind spot?

360
00:24:04,120 --> 00:24:08,900
Mathematically, this missing real direction is identified as a matrix called J tensor J.

361
00:24:09,140 --> 00:24:09,840
Let's untack that.

362
00:24:09,960 --> 00:24:10,480
What is J?

363
00:24:10,700 --> 00:24:13,280
J is the anti-symmetric quarter-turn matrix.

364
00:24:13,660 --> 00:24:15,760
It represents a strict 90-degree rotation.

365
00:24:16,120 --> 00:24:20,380
Now, in real numbers, it is not a valid observable on its own because it is anti-symmetric.

366
00:24:20,480 --> 00:24:21,780
It doesn't have real eigenvalues.

367
00:24:21,780 --> 00:24:30,100
But when you tensor it with itself, when you multiply it across the two systems to get J tensor J, the result suddenly becomes symmetric.

368
00:24:30,640 --> 00:24:33,860
And therefore, it is a valid composite observable.

369
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:37,100
So locally it's invisible, but globally it pops into existence.

370
00:24:37,280 --> 00:24:37,680
Exactly.

371
00:24:37,680 --> 00:24:41,420
Chapter 10 bounds this invisible state to a physical parameter.

372
00:24:41,580 --> 00:24:42,280
Let's call it C.

373
00:24:42,540 --> 00:24:46,860
And the math dictates that C can slide anywhere between negative 1 and 1.

374
00:24:47,260 --> 00:24:57,120
The state of this system is defined as a mixture of the identity matrix, which is just random noise, and this J tensor J term weighted by the parameter C.

375
00:24:57,280 --> 00:24:57,520
Okay.

376
00:24:57,620 --> 00:24:58,560
So we have a valid state.

377
00:24:58,680 --> 00:25:02,780
Now, going back to our detectives, what happens if one detective drops off the case?

378
00:25:03,180 --> 00:25:04,900
We use something called the partial trace, right?

379
00:25:04,960 --> 00:25:06,760
Explain what the partial trace is doing for us here.

380
00:25:06,760 --> 00:25:14,120
The partial trace is simply the mathematical operation you use when you want to ignore one part of a composite system and just look at the other.

381
00:25:14,500 --> 00:25:18,200
You trace over or average out the information of the system you don't care about.

382
00:25:18,400 --> 00:25:26,160
So if I have an entangled pair of particles and one flies off to Mars, I do a partial trace on the Mars particle so I can just calculate what I have in my lab on Earth.

383
00:25:26,700 --> 00:25:27,140
Precisely.

384
00:25:27,140 --> 00:25:31,180
And Chapter 11 proves a concept it calls marginal invisibility.

385
00:25:31,680 --> 00:25:41,540
If you take the partial trace of this specific state, if you look at just one subsystem locally, this entire J tensor J direction vanishes completely.

386
00:25:41,620 --> 00:25:42,260
It just disappears.

387
00:25:42,620 --> 00:25:46,060
Both individual subsystems look like maximally mixed noise.

388
00:25:46,060 --> 00:25:51,480
They look completely random and completely independent of whatever the parameter C happens to be.

389
00:25:51,960 --> 00:25:55,620
So to a local observer, this state looks incredibly boring.

390
00:25:55,940 --> 00:25:58,200
Just pure, uncorrelated static.

391
00:25:58,560 --> 00:26:00,980
You'd never know there was a hidden structure connecting them.

392
00:26:01,060 --> 00:26:03,800
But then we hit the massive paradigm shift in Chapter 12.

393
00:26:04,040 --> 00:26:04,860
We zoom out.

394
00:26:04,960 --> 00:26:06,240
We look at the global state again.

395
00:26:06,240 --> 00:26:07,920
And we ask a simple question.

396
00:26:08,700 --> 00:26:09,960
Is this state entangled?

397
00:26:10,420 --> 00:26:12,000
And the answer is breathtaking.

398
00:26:12,640 --> 00:26:18,540
If Kili C is anything other than exactly zero, the state is provably entangled in real quantum mechanics.

399
00:26:18,820 --> 00:26:23,460
The math shows you cannot build this state simply by mixing independent local states.

400
00:26:23,660 --> 00:26:25,700
The correlation is inherently holistic.

401
00:26:26,140 --> 00:26:26,880
Okay, that makes sense.

402
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:27,120
Yeah.

403
00:26:27,260 --> 00:26:29,380
It has a hidden tenth dimension, so it's entangled.

404
00:26:29,680 --> 00:26:33,340
But wait, this is the part that made me literally drop the manuscript when I read it.

405
00:26:33,360 --> 00:26:34,320
Yes, prepare yourself.

406
00:26:34,320 --> 00:26:41,060
If you look at the exact same mathematical state, the exact same matrix grid of numbers,

407
00:26:41,440 --> 00:26:47,080
but you allow yourself to use complex quantum mechanics to analyze it, it is completely separable.

408
00:26:47,240 --> 00:26:50,240
It is not entangled at all across its entire range of C.

409
00:26:50,420 --> 00:26:53,800
This raises a profoundly important question for physics.

410
00:26:54,540 --> 00:26:59,960
Is entanglement an objective physical reality of the particle out there in the universe?

411
00:26:59,960 --> 00:27:04,400
Or is it merely an artifact of the math we are using to describe it?

412
00:27:04,720 --> 00:27:07,740
The source material proves that separability is field relative.

413
00:27:08,300 --> 00:27:10,140
It is not intrinsic to the state itself.

414
00:27:10,340 --> 00:27:13,700
You are telling me entanglement changes based on the math I choose to use.

415
00:27:13,940 --> 00:27:16,760
It's like looking at one of those magic eye posters from the 90s.

416
00:27:16,780 --> 00:27:17,820
Oh, I love this comparison.

417
00:27:17,820 --> 00:27:21,580
If I wear red-tinted glasses and those glasses represent real quantum mechanics,

418
00:27:22,080 --> 00:27:24,960
I look at the poster and I see a 3D dinosaur popping out of me.

419
00:27:25,440 --> 00:27:27,220
That 3D structure is the entanglement.

420
00:27:27,820 --> 00:27:32,080
But if I take off the red glasses and put on blue-tinted glasses representing complex quantum mechanics,

421
00:27:32,340 --> 00:27:36,060
I look at the exact same poster, and it's just random flat dots.

422
00:27:36,480 --> 00:27:37,260
Separable noise.

423
00:27:37,620 --> 00:27:38,620
The poster didn't change.

424
00:27:38,820 --> 00:27:41,060
The physical matrix on the paper didn't change.

425
00:27:41,360 --> 00:27:42,100
My lens did.

426
00:27:42,100 --> 00:27:46,560
That is exactly what the math dictates, and your analogy captures the mechanism perfectly.

427
00:27:47,500 --> 00:27:52,080
What is local and what is non-local depends entirely on the scalar field permitted by your theory.

428
00:27:52,420 --> 00:27:53,000
But why?

429
00:27:53,320 --> 00:27:55,400
Why does complex QM see it as separable?

430
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:59,920
Because complex QM has a richer set of local states.

431
00:28:00,300 --> 00:28:05,340
It actually has access to that J matrix locally, disguised as the imaginary number I.

432
00:28:05,680 --> 00:28:07,920
Because it has more local building blocks,

433
00:28:07,920 --> 00:28:11,600
it can build this joint state out of purely local pieces.

434
00:28:12,200 --> 00:28:17,180
Real QM lacks those local pieces, so it views the joint state as irreducibly holistic.

435
00:28:17,440 --> 00:28:19,060
It views it as entangled.

436
00:28:19,320 --> 00:28:21,880
It just, it breaks my brain in the best possible way.

437
00:28:22,440 --> 00:28:26,120
The idea that entanglement might just be a symptom of using restricted math.

